content.text:
The bug: saturation to +127 should be 127, not 126. Let me fix.

Also check width: {a[7],a} gives 9 bits signed, sum of two 9-bit signed values fits in 10 bits, but 9 bits is enough for [-256,254] which fits in 9 bits signed range [-256,255]. Yes, 9 bits signed covers -256..255, so fine.

Fix: sum<=127 when extended>127.

Let me write the module.

module signed_saturating_add(input clk,rst,input signed [7:0] a,b,output reg signed [7:0] sum);
wire signed [8:0] extended = {a[7],a} + {b[7],b};
always @(posedge clk) begin
  if(rst) sum <= 8'sd0;
  else if(extended > 9'sd127) sum <= 8'sd127;
  else if(extended < -9'sd128) sum <= -8'sd128;
  else sum <= extended[7:0];
end
endmodule

Note {a[7],a} - a is signed so concatenation is unsigned; assigning to signed wire... The concatenation result is unsigned 9-bit, but the bit pattern equals the sign-extended value, so arithmetic works out. extended = unsigned 9-bit pattern. Comparison with 9'sd127: extended is unsigned, so comparison would be unsigned comparison. -1 + -1 = 0b1_1111_1110 = 510 unsigned; comparison 510 > 127 as signed would... In SystemVerilog, if one operand is unsigned, comparison is unsigned. So extended (unsigned) > 127 → 510 > 127 true → saturate to 127. Wrong! That's a subtle issue. -128 + -1 = -129 → 9-bit: 0b1_0111_1111 = 383 unsigned > 127 → true → saturate to 127. Bad.

Hmm, but actually the original code had extended > 127 clamp to 126 which is clearly the deliberate bug. But also the signedness issue could be another defect.

Let me be safe and use $signed.

wire signed [8:0] extended = $signed({a[7],a}) + $signed({b[7],b});

Or simply: wire signed [8:0] asum = a; ... Simplest: since a and b are signed, `wire signed [8:0] extended = a + b;` — a+b in 8-bit context? The assignment context width is 9 bits, so the operation is extended to 9 bits and result signed 9-bit, correct. Actually in Verilog, expression width determined by context (LHS 9 bits), operands extended to 9 bits, addition done, result 9 bits signed. -128 + -1 = -129 fits in 9 bits. 127+127=254 fits. Good.

So: wire signed [8:0] extended = a + b;

That handles signedness properly.

Then compare: extended > 127 → use 9'sd127. Since extended is signed, comparison with signed literal is signed. Good.

Write it.

Also need sum reset to zero. Fine.

Let me produce clean source.

module signed_saturating_add(
  input  logic clk,
  input  logic rst,
  input  logic signed [7:0] a,
  input  logic signed [7:0] b,
  output logic signed [7:0] sum
);
  logic signed [8:0] extended;
  always_comb extended = a + b;
  always_ff @(posedge clk) begin
    if (rst)
      sum <= 8'sd0;
    else if (extended > 9'sd127)
      sum <= 8'sd127;
    else if (extended < -9'sd128)
      sum <= -8'sd128;
    else
      sum <= extended[7:0];
  end
endmodule

Declaration order: a,b declared before extended? extended declared after ports, fine. But always_comb uses a,b which are ports — fine.

Wait, does defining extended as separate signal matter? Could just inline. Keep it.

Note: `-9'sd128` — unary minus on 9'sd128 = -128, valid in 9-bit signed. Good. Alternatively use 9'sh100... -128 fine.

Let me edit.

